1: E2; Table 2: E1 (with external antenna cable) / E2 (without).\n- Power supply differs: Table 1: AC mains 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz; renewable 6–32 V; replaceable battery 2x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V). Table 2: AC mains ... including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries; renewable ... including 4x 3.6 V; battery: 6x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V).\n\nOther attributes identical: measuring principle, accuracy class 1 and 2, environmental class O, mechanical class M1, temperature range -25/+55 °C, water temperature class T50 (+0.1/+50 °C), MAP 1.6 MPa (16 bar), orientation all positions, flow profile sensitivity U0/D0 except DN250+ U3/D0, reverse flow, pressure loss classes, software ID see table 3/4, checking facilities type P.\n\nApproach: Define models based on transmitter type: \"FEW41Y.Z / FEW43Y.Z\" and \"FEW45Y.Z\". But the schema says one entry per variant. The models are parameterized by wildcards X, Y, Z. The concrete axes:\n- X (electronics variant): 1 (standard), 3 (advanced), 5 (premium MC)\n- Y (version): 1, 2, 8, 9\n- Z (bore): R (reduced), V (virtual full), F (full)\n\nThe tables group FEW41 and FEW43 together, FEW45 separate.\n\nI think the cleanest: model_family.family_name: \"AquaMaster4\", models: the parameterized types. But enumerating 3×4×3 = 36 combos is fabrication-ish; better to record the wildcard axes as attributes.\n\nAlternative approach: use model_level with models being the transmitter type groups as given in tables. Let me structure:\n\nmodel_family:\n  family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n  models:\n    - model_id: \"FEW4XY.Z\", label with wildcard definitions... \n\nHmm, but then attributes varying by model: between Table 1 (X=1,3) and Table 2 (X=5). So model_level entries with models \"FEW41Y.Z / FEW43Y.Z\" and \"FEW45Y.Z\":\n\n- electromagnetic_environment: FEW41/43: E2; FEW45: E1 (with external antenna cable) / E2 (without). Values with conditions... model_level values shape: {model, value, footnote_markers}. The E1/E2 for FEW45 depends