acteristics:\n- power_supply_ac_mains_voltage: {min 95, max 240}, unit \"V\" — same for both → type_level (applies to AC-powered config). Could note it's for AC powered. I'll add at type_level with label power_supply_voltage_ac_mains.\n- power_supply_frequency: {min 50, max 60} Hz.\n- power_supply_dc_renewable_voltage: {min 6, max 32} V.\n- replaceable_battery_cells: FEW41/43: \"2 x 3.6 V\"; FEW45: \"6 x 3.6 V\" — model_level. Value as string \"2 × 3.6 V\"? Or number of cells + cell voltage. Simpler: attribute replaceable_battery_voltage: value \"2 x 3.6 V\" string. Hmm. Let me do:\n  - model_level: attribute \"replaceable_battery_configuration\": values: {model: \"FEW41Y.Z / FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"2 x 3.6 V\"}, {model: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: \"6 x 3.6 V\"}. \n  - battery_operating_range: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6} V — type_level (same in both).\n  - For FEW45, batteries included with AC mains and DC powered options too (\"including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries\"). That's noise; maybe record as part of description. I could add a model_level attribute \"batteries_included_with_ac_dc_supply\": {model FEW45, value \"4 x 3.6 V\"}. Hmm, this is getting noisy. I'll include it since it's factual. Actually rule: don't fabricate but capture present info. I'll include as string values.\n\nModels list: Since tables group them, I'll define models:\n- {model_id: \"FEW41Y.Z / FEW43Y.Z\"} — hmm, one entry per variant. The tables treat FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z identically. I could create three models: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z and in model_level values list FEW41 and FEW43 both with same value. That's more faithful to \"one entry per variant\". Let me do that:\n\nmodels:\n- {model_id: \"FEW41Y.Z\", label: \"AquaMaster4 transmitter, standard variant (X=1)\"}\n- {model_id: \"FEW43Y.Z\", label: \"AquaMaster4 transmitter, advanced variant (X=3)\"}\n- {model_id: \"FEW45Y.Z\", label: \"AquaMaster4 transmitter, premium mobile Comms variant (X=5)\"}\n- {model_id: \"FET4 *\", label: \"Flow sensor..