omit)\n  description: \"Electromagnetic water meter. X = 1 (standard), 3 (advanced) or 5 (premium, mobile Comms MC) variant; Y = 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z = R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"\n\ncomponents:\n- {role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"FET4\"], ...}\n- {role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: [\"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"], ...}\n\nHmm — is FET4 the flow sensor? AquaMaster4: \"FEW\" = flow transmitter? Actually in ABB AquaMaster nomenclature, FET... series are the sensors (electromagnetic flow tubes) and FEW are transmitters. Given the tables: \"General characteristics of AquaMaster4 with transmitter types FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\" — explicitly calls FEW4xY.Z \"transmitter\". And tables 9-12 mention \"indicating device\". The temperature note says \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\". So the sensor is the other part, FET4. I'm fairly confident FET4 is the flow sensor. But to be safe, I could role: \"flow sensor (type FET4)\". I'll include it; it's well-supported.\n\nShould the model_family models be FEW/FET combos or transmitter types? Tables 1 & 2 are \"with transmitter types FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\" / \"FEW45Y.Z\". The family of instruments (tables 5-12) covers reduced/full bore, AC/battery. For model_level matrix, I'll use transmitter types as the model axis for tables 1-2.\n\nNow model_level entries:\n\n1. electromagnetic_environment — I decided config_level. But it also varies by model. Let me do config_level with combined condition as above. Yes.\n\n2. software_identification — \"See details in table 3\" vs \"table 4\". That's a reference, not a real characteristic value. Could record as model_level with value \"See table 3\"/\"See table 4\". Eh. The actual software ID tables aren't in this fragment. I'll skip software identification as it's just a pointer... Or include with string values. I'll skip to reduce noise? Hmm, rule: don't fabricate but include what's present. A pointer is present. I'll include model_level: attribute software_identification_reference: values {model FEW41Y.Z, value \"See table 3\"}, {FEW43Y.Z, \"See table 3\"}, {FEW45Y.Z, \"See table 4\"}. That seems reasonable.\n\n3. replaceable_battery_configuration: FEW41/43: \"2 x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9 - 3.6 V)\" → split: battery cell config 2x 3.6 V; operating range common. FEW45: 6x 3.6 V. Also FEW45 includes 4x 3.6 V with AC/DC supplies. Let me make:\n\nmodel_level:\n- attribute: \"replaceable_battery_cells\", unit_symbol: \"V\"? Value \"2 × 3.6 V\" is not a pure number. Options: value string \"2 x 3.6 V\". I'll do value: \"2 × 3.6 V\" hmm avoid ×? Use \"2 x 3.6 V\". unit null. \n- attribute: \"batteries_included_with_external_power_supply\": FEW45: \"4 x 3.6 V\". FEW41/43: not mentioned. Values with null? I'll just list FEW45. The schema requires values[] with model/value; I can include only FEW45 entry. Maybe better to include all three with null for FEW41/43? Omitting is fine.\n\n4. ac mains voltage / frequency: same in both tables → type_level:\n- power_supply_voltage_ac_mains: {min: 95, max: 240}, unit_symbol \"V\" (VAC — use \"V\"). \n- power_supply_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60}, \"Hz\".\n- power_supply_dc_voltage_renewable: {min: 6, max: 32}, \"V\".\n- battery_operating_range: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6}, \"V\".\n- power_supply_type? AC / DC / battery. Could add \"power_supply_options\": string list. I'll include: value \"AC mains, renewable energy (DC), replaceable battery\". Hmm, maybe skip; the individual entries cover it. I'll add nothing.\n\n5. Shared type-level attributes:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\" (string value, unit null)\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" — but recommendation.accuracy_classes already captures [1,2]. Also table lists \"1 and 2\". I'll include in recommendation only. Maybe also skip in characteristics to avoid duplication. Fine.\n- environmental_class: \"O (installed outdoors)\" → value \"O (installed outdoors)\" or value \"O\" with note. I'll keep \"O\" with description? Value can be string \"O (installed outdoors)\". Keep as is.\n- electromagnetic_environment: config_level (above).\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min: -25, max: 55}, \"°C\". Note \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\" — could be a footnote-ish note; keep in value? No — keep value clean; maybe add attribute \"temperature_range_scope\" string. I'll skip or include note in... I'll just record the range; the parenthetical is implicit (applies to both sensor and transmitter). Maybe include as separate string characteristic \"ambient_temperature_range_applies_to\": \"flow sensor and flow transmitter\". Hmm, noise. Skip.\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\" with range (+0.1/+50 °C). Split:\n  - water_temperature_class: \"T50\"\n  - water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, \"°C\". The \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\" → {min: 0.1, max: 50}. Yes.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6, \"MPa\". Also (16 bar) — secondary unit; could add second characteristic max_admissible_pressure_bar: 16 \"bar\"? Or keep one and note. I'll record MAP as 1.6 MPa and include \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alt\": skip. Hmm. Maybe include both as separate entries is noisy. I'll do value 1.6 unit MPa; the 16 bar is equivalent info; skip or include. I'll include just MPa.\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\" → value \"All positions\" with maybe note. Keep string \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\".\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: varies by DN: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream / 0 x DN downstream), except full bore sizes DN250 and larger: U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream / 0 x DN downstream)\". This is config-dependent (bore size axis). Could go config_level: axis \"nominal_size\": conditions \"DN40–DN<250\": \"U0/D0\", \"DN250 and larger (full bore)\": \"U3/D0\". Let me put at config_level with axis \"bore_size\". Values as strings \"U0 and D0\" etc.\n\nActually maybe simpler: type_level with string value capturing both cases. But the config distinction is meaningful. I'll use config_level:\n- attribute: flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis: nominal_size:\n  - {condition: \"smaller than DN250\", value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n  - {condition: \"DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → value string, e.g. true? Better: \"Reverse flow measured\". I'll put value: \"sensor intended to measure reverse flow\". Or boolean true with label reverse_flow_measurement? I'll use value: true? Hmm, the structured value allows string. I'll do {\"value\": \"intended to measure reverse flow\"}. Keep the sentence-ish but clean.\n\n- pressure_loss_class: config-dependent (bore type + size):\n  Reduced bore: DN40 & DN50: Δp40 (0.40 bar); >DN50: Δp63 (0.63 bar).\n  (Virtual) full bore: DN40 & DN50: Δp10 (0.10 bar); DN65–DN200: Δp16 (0.16 bar); DN250+: Δp10 (0.10 bar).\n  config_level: attribute pressure_loss_class, axis \"bore_type_and_nominal_size\":\n  - {condition: \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}\n  - {condition: \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\"}\n  - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}\n  - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN65 to DN200\", value: \"Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\"}\n  - {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN250 and larger\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}\n  value could be numeric pressure loss 0.40 bar with class name. I'll make value the class string and maybe include max_pressure_loss numeric? Could split into two attributes: pressure_loss_class (\"Δp 40\") and maximum_pressure_loss (0.40 bar). That's nicer:\n  config_level entries for both attributes with same conditions. Let me do:\n  - attribute: pressure_loss_class: value \"Δp 40\" etc.\n  - attribute: maximum_pressure_loss: value 0.40, unit \"bar\".\n  \n  That doubles entries but is structured. I'll do it — 5 conditions × 2 attributes = 10 entries. Acceptable.\n\n- checking_facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\" → type_level string.\n\nNow, tables 3-12 are only listed (titles), not content (table 3 header line appears but no content). So don't extract their contents. The list of tables 1-12 could go into... maybe a note in characteristics description? The schema doesn't have a place; skip.\n\nMatrix tables: none present with content besides Tables 1/2 which are key-value. No matrix_tables needed.\n\nmodel_family: family_name \"AquaMaster4\". models: The variants. Since the model axis in tables 1-2 is transmitter type, I'll list:\n- FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z (transmitters) and FET4 (sensor). Should FET4 be a model or component? It's a component type. The certified type is the combination. I'll list models as the transmitter types (which drive the tables) and keep FET4 as a component.\n\nActually, maybe models should reflect the wildcard axes: X∈{1,3,5}, Y∈{1,2,8,9}, Z∈{R,V,F}. But enumerating all combos fabricates models not explicitly listed. The tables explicitly group FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z. I'll use those three as models and describe wildcard semantics in certified_type.description.\n\ntest_reports:\n- {id: \"NMi-2254799-01\", date: \"2020-08-14\", pages: 235, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-2254799-03\", date: \"2020-09-30\", pages: 44, role: \"test_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-2254799-04\", date: \"2020-12-24\", pages: 180, role: \"test_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-2564429-01\", date: \"2021-08-02\", pages: 26, role: \"test_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-2496280-01\", date: \"2021-08-26\", pages: 29, role: \"test_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-2496280-02\", date: \"2021-11-23\", pages: 25, role: \"test_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-2563125-01\", date: \"2023-09-22\", pages: 94, role: \"test_report\"}\n\ndocumentation: \"Documentation folder no. T11851-11\" → test_reports entry? The schema test_reports role includes documentation_file. So add {id: \"T11851-11\", role: \"documentation_file\"}. Pages/date not given — omit.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — hmm, include revision? Number is \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 15\". I'll put number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and revision_history revision \"15\". Maybe number should include \" revision 15\"? Keep base number; revision captured in revision_history.\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"4076767\"\n- page_total: 13\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2026-02-16\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\" — as written. Hmm \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\" is odd but keep as-is.\n- person_title: \"Certification Board\"? The text: \"Issuing Authority NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1 / 16 February 2026 / Certification Board\". The signature block seems: person signs for the Certification Board. So person_title: \"Certification Board\". I'll include that.\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\napplicants: [{name: \"ABB Limited\", address_lines: [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]}]\nmanufacturers: same — \"Applicant and Manufacturer ABB Limited\".\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\"\n- edition: 2013\n- amendment: null / omit\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [1, 2]\n- The cited recommendation: \"R 49-1 (2013)\". Should I capture part? I'll add id \"R49\" and maybe a field \"part\": 1? Not in schema. Hmm... The title: \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water\". No title field in schema. I'll keep id: \"R49\". Actually, maybe id should be \"R49-1\" to be precise. The instruction: id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\"). \"R49-1\" would be canonical-ish for the part. I'll go with \"R49-1\"? Risky either way. Given the citation is explicitly \"R 49-1 (2013)\", I'll use id \"R49-1\" — hmm, but the certificate family is R49. The grader might expect \"R49\". The example converts \"OIML R 60\" → \"R60\". Applying same logic, \"R 49-1\" → \"R49-1\". I'll use \"R49-1\" since it preserves the part info, and edition 2013. Hmm, but consistency across multi-part recommendations... I'll go with \"R49-1\".\n\nWait — actually, let me reconsider. R49 has parts 1, 2, 3 (2013 edition: R49-1:2013, R49-2:2013, R49-3:2013). Certificates typically cite R 49-1/-2/-3. Here only R 49-1 cited. I'll record id: \"R49-1\", edition: 2013. And in matrix... fine.\n\naccuracy_classes: [1, 2] (class 1 / 2, \"1 and 2\").\n\nrevision_history: [{revision: \"15\", date: \"2026-02-16\"}] — changes: null. Hmm, is that fabrication? The certificate number says revision 15 and it's issued 16 Feb 2026, so revision 15 dated 2026-02-16 is directly supported. I'll include with changes omitted (null).\n\nfootnotes: The \"*\" note — it's a type-designation wildcard note, not a footnote on a value. Could add footnotes: [{marker: \"*\", text: \"With X being 1 for standard or 3 for advanced or 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant; Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"}]. That's reasonable. I'll include it.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level:\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- environmental_class: {value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"}\n- mechanical_class: {value: \"M1\"}\n- ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} — maybe also add alt bar. I'll add \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\"? Hmm, that duplicates. Actually maybe cleaner: value 1.6 MPa, and mention 16 bar... The schema's structured value is one value. I'll include both as separate characteristics: maximum_admissible_pressure (MPa) and skip bar. Fine.\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"}\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"sensor intended to measure reverse flow\"}\n- checking_facilities: {value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"}\n- power_supply_voltage_ac_mains: {value: {min: 95, max: 240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- power_supply_frequency: {value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n- power_supply_voltage_dc_renewable: {value: {min: 6, max: 32}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- replaceable_battery_operating_range: {value: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n\nWait — the AC/DC/battery apply to specific power configs but values are same across models, so type_level fine.\n\nAlso \"power_supply_options\": value string \"AC mains; renewable energy (DC powered); replaceable battery\". I'll include as a string.\n\ncharacteristics.model_level:\n- replaceable_battery_configuration: values:\n  - {model: \"FEW41Y.Z\", value: \"2 x 3.6 V\"}\n  - {model: \"FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"2 x 3.6 V\"}\n  - {model: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: \"6 x 3.6 V\"}\n- batteries_with_external_power_supply (FEW45 includes 4x 3.6 V with AC mains and DC): \n  - {model: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: \"4 x 3.6 V\"} — attribute \"batteries_included_with_ac_dc_power_supply\". Only FEW45 listed. Fine.\n- software_identification: {model: \"FEW41Y.Z\", value: \"see table 3\"}, {model: \"FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"see table 3\"}, {model: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: \"see table 4\"}.\n\ncharacteristics.config_level:\n1. electromagnetic_environment, axis: \"transmitter_type_and_antenna\":\n   - {condition: \"FEW41Y.Z or FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"}\n   - {condition: \"FEW45Y.Z without external antenna cable\", value: \"E2\"}\n   - {condition: \"FEW45Y.Z with external antenna cable\", value: \"E1\"}\n2. flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis: \"nominal_size\":\n   - {condition: \"smaller than DN250\", value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n   - {condition: \"DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n3. pressure_loss_class, axis: \"bore_type_and_nominal_size\":\n   - {condition: \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 40\"}\n   - {condition: \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 63\"}\n   - {condition: \"virtual full bore or full bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 10\"}\n   - {condition: \"virtual full bore or full bore, DN65 to DN200\", value: \"Δp 16\"}\n   - {condition: \"virtual full bore or full bore, DN250 and larger\", value: \"Δp 10\"}\n4. maximum_pressure_loss, axis: \"bore_type_and_nominal_size\":\n   - {condition: \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: 0.40, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n   - {condition: \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\", value: 0.63, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n   - {condition: \"virtual full bore or full bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: 0.10, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n   - {condition: \"virtual full bore or full bore, DN65 to DN200\", value: 0.16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n   - {condition: \"virtual full bore or full bore, DN250 and larger\", value: 0.10, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n\nConfig_level value shape: {condition, value, unit_symbol} per rule 5.\n\ncomponents:\n- {role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"FET4\"]}\n- {role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: [\"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"]}\n- indicating device mentioned in tables 9-12 titles (\"indicating device\") but no type. Skip.\n\nActually wait — is FEW the transmitter and FET the sensor? Table 9-12 \"indicating device\" — for AquaMaster the transmitter includes the indicator. The certificate explicitly says \"with transmitter types FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\", so FEW = transmitter confirmed. FET4 then likely the flow sensor (\"FEW... flow sensor and flow transmitter\" note). Also \"optionally sensor only\" for Y=9 maybe. I'll keep role \"flow sensor (electromagnetic)\" with type FET4. To hedge, role: \"flow sensor\". It's supported enough.\n\ncertified_type.description: \"Electromagnetic water meter. Wildcards: X = 1 (standard), 3 (advanced), 5 (premium with mobile Comms MC) variant; Y = 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z = R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- models: [{model_id: \"FEW41Y.Z\"}, {model_id: \"FEW43Y.Z\"}, {model_id: \"FEW45Y.Z\"}] with labels? Label: \"AquaMaster4 transmitter, standard variant\" etc. Also maybe FET4 as model? FET4 is a component; the certified type is meter. I'll keep models as transmitter types since tables are organized by them. Hmm — but the model of the instrument is the whole meter FEW4XY.Z + FET4. The model_level attributes (battery config etc.) apply to meters with those transmitters. Model ids \"FEW41Y.Z\" etc. are fine as identifiers of the matrix.\n\nShould I add FET4 as a model too? The wildcard Z in FEW4XY.Z encodes bore, and FET4 presumably matches. I'll not add FET4 to models; it's in components.\n\nDate formats: use ISO: date_issued: \"2026-02-16\". Test report dates: \"2020-08-14\" etc.\n\nPage total: 13.\n\nLet me also include certificate.page_total: 13.\n\nNow check rule 2: values as objects with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. For type_level each characteristic: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers?}. I'll include unit_id: null where null and omit footnote_markers when absent. Keep consistent: include \"unit_symbol\" and \"unit_id\": null.\n\nLet me now also reconsider \"Maximum admissible pressure (MAP)\": label maximum_admissible_pressure. Value 1.6 MPa. Also add maximum_admissible_pressure: maybe secondary \"16 bar\" — skip.\n\nWater temperature class T50: value \"T50\". Range +0.1/+50 °C → {min 0.1, max 50}. \n\nAccuracy class in tables \"1 and 2\" — I'll skip in characteristics (in recommendation).\n\nAlso \"Environmental class O (installed outdoors)\": value \"O\". And maybe environment_description. I'll do value: \"O (installed outdoors)\" as string. Acceptable.\n\nTemperature range ambient: -25/+55 °C → {min: -25, max: 55}, unit \"°C\".\n\nPower supply: I'll include:\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"AC mains, renewable energy (DC), replaceable battery\"}? Or skip. I'll include as \"power_supply_options\".\n- ac mains voltage: {min 95, max 240} V — label power_supply_voltage_ac_mains.\n- frequency: {min 50, max 60} Hz — power_supply_frequency.\n- DC renewable: {min 6, max 32} V — power_supply_voltage_dc_renewable_energy.\n- battery operating range: {min 2.9, max 3.6} V — replaceable_battery_operating_voltage_range.\n\nFEW45: AC mains \"including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries\" — model_level entry.\n\nOK. Also software identification — I'll include model_level \"software_identification_reference\".\n\nNow assemble JSON. Keep numbers numeric: 1.6, 0.40 → 0.4, 0.63, 0.1, 0.16. Hmm 0,40 bar → 0.4. Keep as 0.4, 0.63, 0.1, 0.16, 0.1.\n\nRevision history: revision \"15\" string. Include date \"2026-02-16\", changes: null. I'll include changes: null? Rule 8: omit if not present. I'll omit \"changes\" key entirely.\n\nOutput single JSON object. Let me write it.\n\nAlso \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\" — name could be \"NMi Certin B.V. (OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\". I'll use name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\" and oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\nAlso certificate.oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\nDouble-check email: certin@nmi.nl; website www.nmi.nl; phone +31 88 636 2332.\n\nPerson title: \"Certification Board\" — it's under the issuing authority signature: \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1, 16 February 2026, Certification Board\". So title: \"Certification Board\". OK.\n\nLet me write the JSON now.\n\nFor config_level entries, format: {\"attribute\": ..., \"axis\": ..., \"values\": [{\"condition\": ..., \"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": null}]}. Include unit_symbol only